    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d826">

                <pb facs="0887" n="875" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg875"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.5151/190</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the Under Secretary of
                        State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Welles</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-04-09">April 9, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Soviet Ambassador called to see me this afternoon at his request.</p>
                <p>I told the Ambassador that I had received information concerning the newly
                    imposed exchange rate for rubles in so far as the foreign diplomatic corps in
                    Moscow was concerned and I wished to make it clear that I had no protest to make
                    since there was no element of discrimination involved. Nevertheless, the
                    Ambassador would understand that this implied a very great increase in the cost
                    of our representation in Russia. I suggested to the Ambassador that he request
                    the Counsellor of his Embassy<anchor n="69" xml:id="d826-f1"/> to talk with Mr.
                    Henderson about this matter so that we might understand the full
                    implications.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>S[<hi rendition="#small-caps">umner</hi>] W[<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >elles</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d826-f1" n="69" xml:id="d826-f1-content">
                <p>Andrey Andreyevich Gromyko. No record of any subsequent talk on this subject has
                    been found in Department files.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
